Origin:
The firmware can move to another access point of the same network by
itself. net80211 is not told and wpa_supplicant keeps the old keys, so the
link would stay up without traffic. roam_off keeps the firmware on the BSS
it was told to join. This follows from the code; a firmware roam was not
observed on a board.
--- sys/dev/ic/bwfm.c.orig
+++ sys/dev/ic/bwfm.c
@@ -592,6 +592,19 @@ bwfm_init(struct ifnet *ifp)
 		return EIO;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The firmware would move to another access point of the same
+	 * network by itself.  net80211 is not told of such a move and
+	 * wpa_supplicant keeps the keys of the BSS it joined, so the
+	 * handshake the new access point starts is never finished.  Keep
+	 * the firmware on the BSS it was told to join, as Linux does when
+	 * the supplicant is the one that roams; a lost link is reported
+	 * and net80211 scans and joins again.
+	 */
+	if (bwfm_fwvar_var_set_int(sc, "roam_off", 1))
+		printf("%s: could not disable firmware roaming\n",
+		    DEVNAME(sc));
+
 	memset(evmask, 0, sizeof(evmask));
 
 #define	ENABLE_EVENT(e)		evmask[(e) / 8] |= 1 << ((e) % 8)
